From 8a0e462f2b253a9653d699ada225072a16e90698 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Zheng <bluevisor@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 00:14:49 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] app_server: remove offscreen windows' event targets

Desktop::RemoveWindow() is what removes a window's event target from the
EventDispatcher, but it is never called for offscreen (bitmap) windows.
When a view attached to a BBitmap sets an event mask (BScrollBar does so
in its constructor), the offscreen window's target is registered as a
listener and survives the bitmap's deletion. The EventDispatcher then
dereferences the freed target on the next keyboard or pointer event and
app_server's event thread crashes (EventDispatcher::_EventLoop ->
_AddTokens -> BList::ItemAt): input dies and the screen stops redrawing.

Remove the target in ~ServerWindow for offscreen windows as well.
---
 src/servers/app/ServerWindow.cpp | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/servers/app/ServerWindow.cpp b/src/servers/app/ServerWindow.cpp
index fd3f262b..fe0ec932 100644
--- a/src/servers/app/ServerWindow.cpp
+++ b/src/servers/app/ServerWindow.cpp
@@ -208,6 +208,14 @@ ServerWindow::~ServerWindow()
 		fWindowAddedToDesktop = false;
 		fDesktop->RemoveWindow(fWindow.Get());
 		fDesktop = NULL;
+	} else if (fDesktop != NULL) {
+		// Offscreen (bitmap) windows are never added to the desktop, so
+		// Desktop::RemoveWindow() does not remove their event target. A view
+		// in a bitmap that sets an event mask (BScrollBar does in its
+		// constructor) registers that target with the EventDispatcher, which
+		// then keeps a dangling pointer after the bitmap is deleted and
+		// crashes on the next input event.
+		fDesktop->EventDispatcher().RemoveTarget(fEventTarget);
 	}
 
 	if (App() != NULL) {
